# No Sensitive Data in This Repo

Never add real cluster info, passwords, tokens, keys, customer info, or
other sensitive data to any tracked file in this repo -- including docs,
notes, skills, rules, scripts, and commit messages.

This includes (non-exhaustive):

- Kubeconfigs, `kubeadmin-password` files, service account tokens, API keys
- AWS/cloud credentials: access key IDs, secret access keys, account IDs
- Real cluster/domain names, hostnames, or IPs that identify a live or
  specific environment (e.g. `<cluster>.<sandboxNNN>.opentlc.com`)
- Usernames, emails, or console sign-in URLs tied to a specific account
- Private keys, certificates, or ACME account secrets
- **Customer info**: customer/company names, customer contacts, customer
  AWS account IDs or cluster/domain names, support case/ticket numbers,
  Slack/email threads, or any business/PII data tied to a specific
  customer or engagement

## What to do instead

- Keep real environment details in files under `dichen/` (gitignored
  personal notes), never in `.cursor/skills/`, `.cursor/rules/`, or other
  tracked docs.
- In skills/rules/examples, use generic placeholders:
  `<cluster-name>.<base-domain>`, `rhoai-demo.example.com`,
  `<account-id>`, `<access-key-id>`.
- **Never write a real customer/company name anywhere in this repo.** If a
  customer must be referred to at all, use a generic industry placeholder
  instead -- e.g. "XX Bank" or "XX Telecom" -- never their real name.
- Reference credential files by path (`~/.aws/credentials`,
  `auth/kubeconfig`, `pull-secret.json`) rather than embedding their
  contents.
- Add any new local credential/output directories (e.g. `.ocp-install/`)
  to `.gitignore` before they're ever written to, not after.

## Before committing

If a change touches `.cursor/`, docs, or scripts, scan the diff for
account IDs, hostnames matching a real sandbox/cluster pattern, access
keys (`AKIA...`), `BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY`/`CERTIFICATE` blocks, or any
customer/company name, ticket number, or contact info before committing.
When in doubt, ask rather than commit.
